HST Office: the Sheets "Conditional Formatting" dropdown laid out like the familiar office suite.

The Home ribbon button, Format > Conditional and the formatting toolbar dropdown read
sc/uiconfig/scalc/popupmenu/conditional.xml (ResourceMenuController "conditional"), so all three show:
- Highlight Cells Rules: Greater Than, Less Than, Between, Equal To, Text that Contains (the quick dialog,
  .uno:ConditionalFormatEasy?FormatRule), A Date Occurring (the date rule dialog), Duplicate Values (new,
  FormatRule 8: the quick dialog switches between duplicate and unique values).
- Top/Bottom Rules: top/bottom 10 items and 10 %, above/below average (FormatRule 11, 13, 12, 14, 15, 16;
  the count starts at 10).
- Data Bars, Color Scales, Icon Sets: the ready-made presets of DataBarFormatDialog, ColorScaleFormatDialog,
  IconSetFormatDialog?IconSet=n, and "More Rules" (IconSet=-1) opens the rule dialog of that kind. A preset on a
  range that already has a format keeps its other rules and only replaces a rule of its own kind.
- New Rule (ConditionalFormatDialog), Clear Rules from the selected cells or the entire sheet (new slot
  .uno:HstConditionalFormatClear?Scope=0|1, with undo), Manage Rules.
The quick dialog lists ready-made styles first ("Light Red Fill with Dark Red Text" by default, yellow, green);
the chosen one becomes a cell style of the document on first use (one undo step with the rule), and .xlsx
export writes it as the dxf of the rule. Its description no longer reads "values values".

